WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:20.199
Hi. My name is Frank Schulenburg and I'm the Wikimedia Foundation's

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.599
Head of Public Outreach. The Wikimedia Foundation is the organization

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.466
behind Wikipedia, the encyclopedia that everybody can edit.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:35.966
So I'm here today to tell you more about Wikipedia and

00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:41.566
why you as a science student should help Wikipedia to get better.

00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:48.332
Let me start with a story about what impact Wikipedia can have.

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:56.632
This is the story of Judy. And Judy is the mother of a 20 year old daughter

00:00:56.633 --> 00:01:02.166
from Florida and Judy's daughter was diagnosed with cancer

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:09.999
at the age of 2. So 18 years ago, and at that time, Judy spent hours

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.632
in medical libraries to get more information about the specific type

00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:21.732
of cancer that her daughter had. And 18 years ago, I mean,

00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:24.166
she could find little information

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:29.166
about that specific type of cancer. She couldn't understand

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:35.266
the medical terms she was reading. However, her daughter survived.

00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:41.299
And then, 12 years later, Judy's daughter was diagnosed with

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:47.766
a different kind of cancer and this time Judy used the internet

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:53.399
and Wikipedia. She was armed with knowledge about the disease,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:00.099
its causes, and possible treatments. And she sent us an email last year

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:05.566
when we were in the middle of our yearly fundraiser, and Judy said

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:11.532
"Wikipedia and the internet has helped me to gain a sense of control

00:02:11.533 --> 00:02:20.299
over the uncontrollable." I think this is a really nice story to see

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:28.299
how Wikipedia impacts the lives of people. So let me tell you more

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.266
about Wikipedia, let me give you some numbers, and let me tell you

00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:37.599
more about who's behind Wikipedia, who writes it. Let's start with

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.866
the numbers. Wikipedia right now has 18 million articles in more

00:02:44.867 --> 00:02:53.466
than 260 languages. And what I find really impressive is that Wikipedia

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:57.966
worldwide, all of these language versions, get more than

00:02:57.967 --> 00:03:06.732
400 million unique visitors per month. And all the content of Wikipedia

00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:15.832
is written by volunteers. People like you and me. There are more

00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:22.266
than 100,000 of volunteers who write Wikipedia articles, and they

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:27.066
don't get paid. They just do it because they like to learn

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:31.999
and they like to teach others and they like to share their knowledge

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.966
with others. And they all believe in the same vision, that vision statement

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:43.666
of the Wikimedia foundation, that is, we believe that it's good to create

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:49.832
a world where every single human being on this planet has access

00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:57.766
to the sum of all knowledge. And now, back to the topic. So I said

00:03:57.767 --> 00:04:01.932
I want to give you some reasons why you as science students

00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:05.766
should help Wikipedia to get better. And I have three reasons.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:14.732
First of all, if you write Wikipedia articles, you will learn a lot.

00:04:14.733 --> 00:04:22.266
You will learn to translate your special knowledge to a general audience.

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:29.199
To an audience of people like Judy, who needs information and

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.566
who needs information that is written in an easy to understand language.

00:04:34.567 --> 00:04:42.432
So you will also learn to write better. You will improve your writing skills,

00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:47.532
because you will interact with the community of Wikipedia editors

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:50.966
and the readers, and you will get feedback, and people will tell you

00:04:50.967 --> 00:04:54.566
"Hey, there is something that I didn't understand, and maybe you

00:04:54.567 --> 00:05:00.032
can write it in a different way." So that feedback is really good.

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:04.299
And then also what you will learn is, you will improve

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:09.699
your digital media literacy skills, because when you write

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:15.466
Wikipedia articles, you will learn a lot about Wikipedia's culture and

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:20.166
you will learn a lot about what makes a good article and why are some

00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:26.132
articles not as good as other articles, so you will learn a lot about

00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:33.766
how the quality of Wikipedia, how Wikipedia gets created and that's

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:40.332
one of the big learnings. So the second reason why I think you as a

00:05:40.333 --> 00:05:45.232
science student should help Wikipedia to get better is, you can give

00:05:45.233 --> 00:05:52.332
something back. So I guess that most of you use Wikipedia

00:05:52.333 --> 00:05:59.232
at least once a week. And let me tell you about me, I use

00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:04.999
Wikipedia every day and five years ago I decided I wanted to give

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.999
something back to Wikipedia. So I studied history and five years ago

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.832
I started to improve history articles on Wikipedia, and it gives me

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:20.599
a good feeling because I'm taking information from Wikipedia every day.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:25.266
I learn a lot, I enjoy reading Wikipedia articles, so I give something back.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:31.166
So the third reason why I think you as a science student should help

00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:37.666
Wikipedia to get better is that huge audience that Wikipedia has.

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:42.966
Imagine the cancer article on the English Wikipedia gets

00:06:42.967 --> 00:06:49.666
more than 200,000 views per month. Isn't that amazing?

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:55.732
So many views? I remember when I wrote articles for scientific journals,

00:06:55.733 --> 00:07:04.432
maybe I got 20 readers or so. But on Wikipedia, my articles reach

00:07:04.433 --> 00:07:09.299
much more people, and this is a great thing. So imagine you would

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.099
help to make the cancer article better, and you would get

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.399
more than 200,000 views per month. Wouldn't that be great?

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:24.566
So here's the three reasons again, you will learn a lot,

00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:30.032
you can give something back to Wikipedia, and you will write

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:37.699
for a huge audience. And now there are many ways you can help Wikipedia

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:44.166
to get better. You can just take a look at an article and look at

00:07:44.167 --> 00:07:48.366
the language, and maybe you want to copy-edit it. It's very easy.

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:52.532
You just click on the edit button, and you can start copy-editing

00:07:52.533 --> 00:07:55.566
and then you click save and everything is done and you've helped

00:07:55.567 --> 00:08:00.399
Wikipedia to get better. You can also fact check Wikipedia articles,

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.532
existing Wikipedia articles, or you can upload pictures or you can even start

00:08:05.533 --> 00:08:11.532
a new Wikipedia article, and it's really easy. Go to Wikipedia,

00:08:11.533 --> 00:08:17.199
click on the edit button and then start editing. And you can help

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:23.766
people like Judy to find the information that they need.

00:08:23.767 --> 01:00:00.000
Thank you very much.

